A global credential-compromise campaign linked by researchers to Zestix, also known as Sentap, targeted enterprise cloud file-sharing environments using credentials previously stolen by information-stealing malware. Reported victim environments included ShareFile, ownCloud, and Nextcloud across organizations in sectors including healthcare, government, aviation, defense, utilities, telecommunications, transportation, legal services, and other industries. The campaign is especially important because a software vulnerability was not required in documented cases: valid credentials obtained through infostealers such as RedLine, Lumma, and Vidar could be used against accounts where effective multi-factor authentication was absent. The result was authenticated access to sensitive corporate repositories followed by data theft. The incident demonstrates that an organization can be fully patched and still be compromised when stolen credentials remain valid and identity controls fail to stop their reuse.
